# wfg-log 1
# fixture waits — a pre-wait and a post-wait, hand-written and hand-checked.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR. A wait is a deadline, and a deadline is the easiest
# thing in an engine to get right live and wrong on replay: the obvious way to
# write one is a counter in a tick hook, and `wfg replay` runs no hooks at all.
# A cue with a two-second pre-wait would then sit in `waiting` for the whole of
# the replayed session while the live one fired it perfectly.
#
# So a wait coming due is a COMMAND — `run.fire` at the near end, `run.done` at
# the far one — and this file is what that buys: every wait below elapses
# because the log says it did, on the tick the log says, with no clock of its
# own in the room.
#
# It needs the bundle because GO reads standby out of the show. It needs no
# audio side: `Audio tracks="0"` is a real answer rather than a placeholder, and
# a show of memos is exactly the show a designer writes on a train.
#
# THE FIRST CUE HAS A PRE-WAIT of 0.2 s, which is ten ticks. GO creates the run
# at once — a cue somebody can see coming, rather than a gap where a cue should
# be — and standby advances immediately (§3.5), whatever the cue then does.
A 0 0 cli go s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# Ten ticks later the wait is over and the engine tells itself so. The run
# leaves `waiting` and the cue fires.
A 10 1 engine run.fire s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# A memo has nothing to do, so it ends on the next tick — the same tick a
# network cue with `wait: none` ends on, and for the same reason: that is when a
# report is allowed to leave, not because anything was waited for. With no
# post-wait it goes straight to `done`.
A 11 2 engine run.ended s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# THE SECOND CUE HAS A POST-WAIT of 0.3 s, fifteen ticks, and no pre-wait. So it
# fires on the GO itself and there is no `run.fire`.
A 20 3 cli go s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# Its own work is over on the next tick — and it is NOT done. §3.6 makes a
# post-wait "how long after completion this cue reports done to its parent", so
# the run moves to `postWait` and anything holding on it keeps holding.
A 21 4 engine run.ended s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# Fifteen ticks after that, it reports done. This record is the whole reason
# `run.done` exists rather than a timer somewhere.
A 36 5 engine run.done s:"H2K4M6NP"
#
# THE THIRD CUE HAS BOTH, five ticks each, and they do not run into each other:
# the pre-wait, then the cue, then the post-wait, in that order.
A 50 6 cli go s:"Q6RS8TW2"
A 55 7 engine run.fire s:"Q6RS8TW2"
A 56 8 engine run.ended s:"Q6RS8TW2"
A 71 9 engine run.done s:"Q6RS8TW2"
#
# THE FOURTH CUE WAITS TEN SECONDS, and nobody is going to wait for it.
A 80 10 cli go s:"V3XY5Z7B"
#
# Killed in its pre-wait, from a surface. This is the case that had no owner: a
# waiting run holds no voice, no job and no level, and `run.kill` writes
# `stopping` over the `waiting` that said who was looking after it. Before the
# Runner swept for it, the run stayed `stopping` until the show closed — and a
# group holding on it would have held for ever.
A 90 11 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 run.kill s:"V3XY5Z7B"
A 91 12 engine run.ended s:"V3XY5Z7B"
#
# And a wait that came due for a run nobody created is refused rather than
# inventing one to fire. `run.fire` may be sent by anyone — a command only the
# inside of the process could send would be one a replay could not send — so it
# has to answer for an argument it does not recognise.
R 100 13 cli unknown-id run.fire s:"NOSUCHID"
